  "formal_question": "The diagram shows a shape made from ten squares of side-length $1 \\mathrm{~cm}$, joined edge to edge. What is the length of its perimeter, in centimetres?",
  "material_difference": "Old Plane433 was screened without a saved candidate/API because a subset of seams alone gave no unit counterpart and could cue an arbitrary omission. New design records all11 actual contacts once each and pairs them with one real outer1cm side. The specific representation error is one geometric contact record versus TWO incident side occurrences in4*10. Wrong40-11=29 is derived before tests. No original edge is moved or erased, no new closed cell is drawn, and no inner seam is claimed exterior.",
  "primary_unique_math_proof": "Exact native polygon-edge incidence yields10 squares,11 edges with two owners and18 exterior edges with one owner. Summing all40 side occurrences, each shared contact has contributed twice, so P=40-2*11=18cm. This is unique and can also be checked by traversing the complete outer silhouette. A single contact record is a physical one-unit segment but corresponds to two cell-side occurrences; subtracting once gives29. All red contact lines lie exactly on existing seams, and the one exterior exemplar is separately identified by its one-owner topology, not a new mathematical condition.",
  "human_semantics": "Numerical perimeter of the same ten-square union. Thin fixed1pt red overlays coincide exactly with existing grid edges; they add no cells or cuts and retain the entire black exterior outline except the one actual exterior unit counterpart. Cells remain joined. Red contact lines are internal under the same incidence topology; color does not redefine the perimeter target.",
